course: dictionary
lesson: 1864
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Coin is one of them: it can be a thing you hold, an action you do, or a way of making a new word. The sentence around the word tells you which meaning is right. «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts» is about money, because you count the coins and find their value.
explain: Look for clues. «Coins thrown into a wishing fountain are usually collected for charity» tells you that coins are money people throw in. To toss a coin means to throw it up and see whether it lands on heads or tails. To coin a word means to make up a brand-new word.
explain: Try each meaning in the sentence. If "a small, flat, round piece of metal" fits, the noun meaning is right. If "throw it up and see" fits, the verb meaning is right. Always check that your choice makes sense in the whole sentence.
fact: A coin is a small, flat, round piece of metal used as money.
fact: To toss a coin is to throw it up and see whether it lands on heads or tails.
fact: To coin a word is to make up a new word.
fact: In Australia the $1 and $2 coins are gold-coloured, so people call them "gold coins", though they contain no gold.
fact: In Canada the one-dollar coin is called a loonie, because it has a loon on it, and the two-dollar coin is called a toonie.
exercise: «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts.» In this sentence, what is a coin? | A bird's nest | *A small, flat, round piece of metal used as money | A tall tower on a castle | Bag for carrying shoes
exercise: Ollie and his friend toss a coin to decide who goes first. What does toss mean here? | To hide it under a cup | *To throw it up and see if it lands on heads or tails | To buy a snack with it | To drop it down a drain
exercise: Mia says, "I will coin a word for my new game." What does coin mean here? | *To make up a new word | To win a prize | To count out money | To spend all her money
test: «In Canada the one-dollar coin is called a loonie, because it has a loon on it.» Why is it called a loonie? | It is the same size as a bird | It was made by a bird | *It has a loon on it | It is the colour of a bird's egg
test: Which sentence uses coin as a verb, meaning to make up? | Mum found a coin under the sofa | The coins are in the jar | I put a coin in the slot | *Can you coin a name for your new pet?
exam: Ali has a 25-cent quarter and a 10-cent dime. How many cents does Ali have altogether? | 45 | 15 | *35 | 25
